## Gate record

**20 attempts across five gates; 6 passed, 14 did not** (8 `rerun`,
6 `escalated`), and 9 recorded seat BLOCK verdicts. Counted by reading
`refs/etude/runs/etude-9uf.4:manifest.json`, not by hand.

| phase | attempts | outcomes |
|---|---|---|
| `plan` | 2 | pass ×1, rerun ×1 |
| `implement` | 3 | pass ×1, rerun ×2 |
| `verify` | 10 | escalated ×6, pass ×3, rerun ×1 |
| `docs` | 4 | pass ×1, rerun ×3 |
| `review` | 1 | rerun ×1 |

Every `escalated` attempt is the `opus` seat having no working subprocess path
(bead `etude-8li`) — `etude gate` failing closed on an incomplete panel, which is
the machine behaving correctly rather than a finding.

**The verify phase records three passes, and that is deliberate history rather
than a bookkeeping oddity.** Each certified a different revision: `verify.r4`
passed `bd2d9d94`, `verify.r7` passed `ea641e95`, and `verify.r11` passed the
revision that ships. The first two were superseded because I folded the seat's
optionals AFTER its verdict — which quietly turns a pass into a certificate for
something else. The final review blocked on exactly that, twice. The last round
was therefore frozen before the seat read it, and its three optionals were filed
as bead `etude-le0` rather than applied. All twenty attempts are kept, including
the superseded passes: deleting them would make the record tidier and less true.

### The gate record was itself incomplete, and the final review caught it

An earlier version of this artifact reported 12 attempts and claimed the count
was "from the manifest", while its own findings table cited "verify r1" and
"verify r2" rounds **that existed in no gate record**. Two substantive in-harness
opus BLOCKs in the verify phase had been acted on — they changed the code, taking
the suite from 34 to 40 to 45 cases — and never appended. Manifest-wide there
were seven `block` verdicts and not one in a verify gate, so `etude run show`
reported that phase as escalated/escalated/pass.

Worse, the only recorded verify PASS certified artifact `bd2d9d94`, while the
optionals folded after that verdict produced `ea641e95` — so **the revision that
ships had never been gated**. Re-pointing the old record would have been the
dishonest fix; the shipped revision was gated for real instead, and both seats
passed it.

This is the same defect that blocked bead `.3` five times, reproduced here after
I wrote a retro about it. The cause is structural and unchanged: the `opus` seat
cannot be invoked as a subprocess, so `etude gate` never produces its attempt,
and appending it is a separate manual step — easiest to skip exactly when the
verdict is interesting enough to act on immediately. `etude-4ed` is the real fix.
Until it lands, no discipline I have applied has been sufficient two beads
running.

## What the gates found

This bead SHRANK a safety check that sits on every lane's push path, and that is
exactly when fail-opens get written. The gates found **eight**, plus three holes
where a check existed but nothing tested it:

| Where | Defect |
|---|---|
| implement r1 | a run ref with no readable `manifest.json` exited **0, clean** on a hard check — `pipefail` plus an `\|\| echo -1` fallback appending to a sentinel already printed |
| implement r1 | `git ls-remote` failure swallowed by `\|\| true` — an unreachable origin became a pile of "unpushed" gaps and exit 1 instead of exit 2 |
| implement r2 | a `case` with no default arm |
| implement r2 | `gates` counted by `len()` with no type check |
| implement r2 | `mapfile < <(python3 …)` discarding its producer's status — a truncated window read as clean |
| implement r2 | `for-each-ref` failure making check (d) pass vacuously |
| verify r2 | `for-each-ref` guard downgradeable to `\|\| true` with the suite green |
| verify r2 | a python3 failure defaulting to "one gate" — every run would have recorded as gated |
| verify r1 | **half of check (d) untested**: every case minted only run refs, so deleting `refs/etude/retros` from its sweep left 34/34 green |
| verify r1 | the fail-closed `case` arm untested — the instance was pinned, the class was not |
| verify r1 | `cd "$REPO_ROOT"` untested, and the test written for it could not fail |

The first was found independently by both seats and reproduced before fixing. The
902-line predecessor handled it correctly, so it was a regression this bead
introduced — into the very check whose job is not to fail open.

**The verify seat did not read the tests, it mutated the script.** That is what
found the three coverage holes, and every pin was then verified by re-running its
mutation. A regression never seen to fail is a guess.

## What the docs gate found

Four rounds, three BLOCKs, every one on the artifact claiming a file was clean
when it was not — and twice on errors the sweep itself had introduced.

Deleting a check invalidates every document that promised the guarantee, and
correcting those documents can be wrong in **both** directions:

- asserting a guarantee that is gone — `docs/retro.md` still said the sidecar
  "**is checked by** the dogfood audit script" three sentences below a bold
  "Nothing enforces it now";
- past-tensing a behavior that survives — I wrote that the audit's superseded-set
  was "the pattern used before `etude-9uf.4` shrank it" when the shrunk audit
  still builds it, in a comment 22 lines above another comment I wrote saying it
  still uses it.

The second is harder to catch because the sentence looks appropriately humble.

**Nothing mechanical in this repo detects either.** `make docs-check` and
`make docs-reality` were green through all four rounds and neither reads prose. A
seat reading the text is the only check there is, and that is worth knowing given
this bead just removed a documentation-drift check as redundant.

## Honest limits

- **Nothing forces `etude gate`.** (a) and (b) DETECT a skipped model; they do
  not prevent one. That gap justified keeping them and is not closed here.
- **Check (b) counts gate ATTEMPTS, not passing verdicts.** A run whose gates all
  BLOCKed satisfies it.
- **Check (c) measures retro REFS, lanes write retro MARKDOWN** — `etude-3xt`.
  It is WARN-only for that reason.
- **The 7-key sidecar convention is now unenforced** while `retro-meta-index.sh`
  still consumes it — `etude-k47`.
- **Check (d) does not cover `refs/etude/evals/*`**, which `etude sync` pushes.
  Pre-existing scope, unchanged here.
- **Check (c)'s coverage computation is unpinned** — deletable with the suite
  green. WARN-only, and `etude-3xt` owns what it measures.
- **The audit needs bash 4+** (`declare -A`, `mapfile`, `[[ -v ]]`), so
  macOS's `/bin/bash` 3.2 cannot run it. Pre-existing — the predecessor used
  eleven such constructs to this one's five — but previously undisclosed.
- **The audit needs the network**, since check (d) calls `git ls-remote`. An
  unreachable origin is now correctly exit 2 rather than a false gap.

